in three separate strikes  israel again showed what it can do at  will to gaza's skyline  the al-sharook tower contains shops  offices and homes  and on its top floor the  hamas-affiliated al-aqsa tv  occupants had been told by israel to  leave  seconds after the attack began the  building's silhouette  replaced by smoke and dust  across gaza a cloud of grief is  spreading  taking hold of families infecting a new  generation  [Music]  as the deaths mount so does the feeling  that this is just the beginning  my daughter-in-law and my grandson died  and my granddaughter is missing without  any trace  the jews target women and children my  son's wife was five months pregnant  this is terrorism  less than an hour after the al-sharukh  building was brought down  another rocket barrage began  again israel's iron dome interceptors  trying to deal with a huge number of  targets  hamas said it fired 130 rockets several  got through  to the town of sterot a six-year-old boy  killed  in one direct hit on a building  others exploding in the coastal town of  ashkelon  earlier israel killed six senior hamas  commanders  close to the head of the military wing  and involved in intelligence and rocket  production  a short while ago we eliminated top  commanders of the hamas general staff  including the gaza city brigade  commander and other commanders  and this is just the beginning we'll hit  them like they've never dreamed possible  but there is another growing crisis for  benjamin netanyahu and his government  away from this accelerating military  escalation  in and around gaza across israel from  town to town  there is an eruption of communal  violence  between jews and arabs despite  netanyahu's  own pledge to bring it to an end with an  iron fist  on tuesday night jewish cars and a  synagogue were torched  on wednesday it was a mosque that burned  [Applause]  elsewhere arab businesses were ransacked  jewish crowds chanted death to arabs  communities used to living close to each  other doing business with each other  driven by violence and hatred israel  facing a social and security crisis on  its streets  as it wages an escalating conflict with  gaza's fighting factions  harry fawcett al-jazeera southern israel  well robbie curry is the director of  global engagement at the american  university of beirut joins me now via  skype from boston in the us mr curry  always good to have you on the programme  um we've seen this huge surge really of  intercommunal violence which harry  mentioned in his report between jewish  and arab communities  across parts of israel that we would  never have heard of before  how much of a game changer is this now  i don't know if inter-communal violence  is the best way to describe it it's  predominantly  israelis private sector gangs  often protected by the police um  attacking  palestinians and then after a while the  palestinians start  fighting back so the pattern is that the  israeli government  and the the the gangs of young men  have been working for years and years to  try to judaize most of  palestine as much as they can they can't  judaize at all because there's  several million there's about five  million palestinians living  west of the jordan river and the  occupied territories and  israel proper but the the the plan  that the israelis the actions they've  always used have been to  slowly push out palestinians um like  using uh legal means  like military actions that destroy homes  and people  try to get people to flee either out of  fear or simply out of not being able to  live normally anymore  and then the third one is to incite the  local fear and violence among  small groups of people maybe one family  maybe a neighborhood  and and get the palestinians to go out  the striking thing about this is that  this has been the pattern  since around 1920. this is not  just last week or since 67 or since 48.  this is how the palestinian israeli  the zionist arabist conflict started  way back in the 1920s as small groups of  jews  organized and planned after the balfour  declaration  in 1917 planned to create a jewish  homeland  and a jewish state and to do that they  had to push out as many palestinians as  possible this has been documented by  israeli scholars international agencies  a lot of people so this is the context  in which we're seeing  this violence now the palestinians fight  back  in any way they can but they're way  outnumbered and outgunned  by the israeli government and the and  the private folks  so this is what you end up with so i  mean here and now then how do you think  the israeli  authorities are going to deal with these  clashes between arabs and israeli jews  i say west of the jordan river in places  uh  like haifa accra  these clashes these clashes very much  serve the interests of the ruling  right-wing majority including netanyahu  right now and in fact many of these  groups that  carry out these actions belong to  parties that are  among the right-wing militant coalitions  coalition group so the israeli  government will make noises about  protecting all the citizens but they  will just  let the violence continue and the  government itself  conducts much of the violence either  through military actions  or through the legal system so i  you i don't think you're going to see  any short-term  serious action by the israeli government  to to reign  rein in these gangs who go around  beating up arabs or killing them or  breaking into their  houses they want to continue this has  been the tool  of choice violence terrorizing  frightening tormenting  families and neighborhoods to gradually  increase the jewish population  and decrease the palestinian population  they'll stop it for a week or two  maybe with a talk on the white house uh  phone call  and then it'll it'll resume and it  resumes by legal means  and military means on political means  and  and private violence means so this is  the real  problem that the palestinians face they  have this  powerful israeli state supported at the  beginning of this process a century ago  by the united kingdom and france a  little bit now supported by the united  states so there's no way that the  palestinians  can fight back against this in any  serious way so the solution is a  political  resolution that's the only solution  really that we should aim for  we should see what does happen it's  always good to get your insight randy  curry thanks so much for joining us from  boston thank you

